    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Five Card Stud',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Five Card Stud is one of the earliest forms of Stud Poker, emerging in the mid-19th century. Unlike Draw Poker, Five Card Stud is an 'open' game where most of a player's hand is visible to everyone at the table. Each player receives one hidden 'hole' card and four exposed 'up' cards, with betting rounds interspersed between each deal. This creates a high-tension environment where players must calculate their strength against the visible threats in their opponents' hands.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Five Card Stud rose to prominence during the American Civil War, evolving from earlier forms of Poker as a way to provide more information and betting opportunities per hand. It was the standard casino Poker variant for decades before being largely supplanted by Seven Card Stud in the mid-20th century.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2 to 10 players. Best with 5 to 7.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards are dealt one at a time. The first card is face-down, subsequent cards are face-up.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Fifth card face down: The fifth card is dealt face down so only three cards are visible in the last betting round; first to act in that round is the same as the previous round.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Lowball: Lowest hand wins. Ace counts low. First betting round often started by highest card showing (bring-in); subsequent rounds by lowest hand showing. Standard low hand ranking (e.g. ace-to-five or ace-to-six) used at showdown.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Five Card Stud High-Low: Pot split between highest and lowest hands; declaration (high or low) after final betting; odd chip to high. Often 8-or-better for low.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Sökö (California Stud): Same as Five Card Stud except two extra hand ranks below a pair: 4-straight (four consecutive ranks plus odd card) and 4-flush (four of one suit plus odd card). Hand order: high card, pair, 4-straight, 4-flush, two pair, three of a kind, straight, flush, full house, four of a kind, straight flush.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Five Card Stud is one of the earliest forms of Stud Poker, emerging in the mid-19th century. Unlike Draw Poker, Five Card Stud is an 'open' game where most of a player's hand is visible to everyone at the table. Each player receives one hidden 'hole' card and four exposed 'up' cards, with betting rounds interspersed between each deal. This creates a high-tension environment where players must calculate their strength against the visible threats in their opponents' hands.",
