        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by having the best 5-card poker hand or by being the last player remaining after everyone else folds.',
          gameplay: '1. 1st Street: Each player gets one card face-down (hole) and one card face-up. Betting round begins with the lowest upcard (Bring-In).\n2. 2nd Street: A second upcard is dealt. Betting round begins with the highest visible hand.\n3. 3rd Street: A third upcard is dealt. Betting round begins with the highest visible hand.\n4. 4th Street: The final upcard is dealt. Final betting round.\n5. Showdown: hole cards are revealed.',
          keyRules: [
            'The Bring-In: Unlike blinds, the first bet is forced on the player with the worst visible start (the lowest card).',
            "Street nomenclature: The rounds are often called 'Third Street' (the first 2 cards) through 'Fifth Street' (the final card).",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Fold early. If your hole card and first up-card don't form at least a high pair or a very strong high-card combination, you should rarely see the third card.",
          intermediate: "The 'Upcard' Check: Always look at EVERY upcard on the table. If you are drawing for a Flush in Hearts, and you see three other Heart cards in your opponents' upcards, your odds of completing your hand are significantly reduced.",
          advanced: "Representing a Hand: If you have a high upcard (like an Ace or King), you can bet aggressively to represent a 'Pair of Aces'. Opponents must respect the possibility that your hole card matches your visible high card.",
          tips: [
            "If an opponent's visible upcards already beat your entire 5-card potential, fold immediately.",
            'Position matters less than board texture. A player with two visible Kings is the lead, regardless of their seat position.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
